{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792332743,"nonce":"xMENj6HvMUQwH7iMlkaRmSRZdzE=","title":"Extensions: HXX as a dynamic scripting language on a Rust bytecode interpreter (draft plan)","message":"Status: draft for the user's review. Nothing is built. Facts below were read from the code and docs of ashui, ash, zyntax and hl_abi on 2026-10-18; what is not verified is listed under \"Unknowns\".\n\n\nAn extension is a folder with a manifest and scripts written in an HXX-flavoured language. The scripts hold logic and presentation together: components with state, markup literals, event handlers, effects. RayDE loads them at run time, with no Haxe compile, and they add commands, key bindings, settings, colour schemes, status items and views (a page like Settings, a sidebar, a panel). A bytecode interpreter in Rust runs them, off the UI thread, in a sandbox.\n\nNot goals now: a marketplace, extensions that replace the editor, language services (LSP-style providers; the plan leaves room for them).\n\n\n- HXX is a compile-time macro (ashui/haxe/ashui/ui/Hxx.hx, on tink_hxx's parser). A tag lowers to a constructor plus `node.set(Prop.X, v)`; a kebab-case tag names a typed `Component` class and each attribute is checked against its props. None of that exists at run time.\n- `class=\"p-4 flex\"` utilities are resolved by `Tw.setters`, which is `#if macro` only. A script needs a run-time resolver over the same table.\n- Reactivity is wrapped by type: an expression that calls `.get()` on a signal becomes a `Computed`. A dynamic language has no types to decide that, so every `{expr}` is wrapped. This is fine: ashui's `Computed` tracks reads dynamically, so only the decision moves.\n- Natives in this stack never call back into HashLink. hl_abi declares no closure call, and the terminal, hlwindow and ash-future all poll. A script runtime that calls Haxe closures would be the first, and would need ash support.\n\n\n**One boundary, an op stream.** The script runtime owns its own reactive graph (signals, computeds, effects) and its element tree as data. Each frame it hands the host a batch of ops: create element (id, tag), set prop (id, name, value), set text, insert/move/remove child, attach/detach event listener. The Haxe side applies them to ashui elements through a tag registry. Events go back as messages: element id, event name, payload; the runtime runs the handler and emits the patches it causes. The same shape as the terminal: the VM polls, takes a batch, applies it.\n\nWhy this and not shared closures or shared objects:\n- No HL closure crosses the boundary, which the ABI cannot do today.\n- The extension never holds a Haxe object, so there is nothing to leak or corrupt, and a script cannot block the UI thread.\n- Fine-grained patches follow from fine-grained reactivity: a changed signal updates one text node, not a subtree.\n- The stream can be recorded and replayed: snapshot tests without a window, and a trace for agents (describe, structured errors).\n\n**Runs on its own thread** (an extension host). Each extension gets a fuel budget per frame slice and a memory cap; running out stops that extension and shows a structured error on the Extensions page, with the source span. A panic is caught the same way.\n\n**Capabilities, not ambient authority.** A script sees nothing but what the manifest asks for and the user allowed: `workspace.read`, `workspace.write`, `process.spawn` (with an allow-list), `net.fetch` (host list), `terminal`, `clipboard`. Each is a host function that exists only if granted.\n\n\nHaxe's expression and class syntax, so people and agents already know it, plus markup literals as expressions and four reactive forms. A sketch, not a spec:\n\n    import rayde.workspace as ws;\n\n    @command(\"wordcount.show\", key: \"cmd+alt+w\")\n    function show() panel.open(\"wordcount\");\n\n    component WordCount {\n      state words = 0;\n      effect words = ws.activeBuffer.text.split(\" \").length;\n      view \u003cdiv class=\"flex items-center gap-2 px-3\"\u003e\n        \u003cicon name=\"file-text-line\"/\u003e\n        \u003ctext\u003e{words} words\u003c/text\u003e\n        \u003cbutton onClick={() -\u003e ws.say(\"hello\")}\u003eSay hello\u003c/button\u003e\n      \u003c/div\u003e\n    }\n\nDecisions to settle in M0/M2: gradual typing (annotations optional, checked where present, so tag and prop mistakes are found at load time, as HXX does today) or fully dynamic; no macros, no reflection, no implicit globals.\n\n\nA. **A frontend on zyntax** (a .zyn grammar). Zyntax already has: a grammar-defined language path (\"a new language needs no Rust\"), a HIR interpreter with tiered JIT behind it, a `parse-only` feature that builds without Cranelift, host modules (typed classes and functions the embedder exposes), a dynamic value box (`DynamicBox`, used by its Lua frontend), algebraic effects and fibers, a `.zbc` bytecode format with a checksum, and hot reload at call, yield and loop boundaries. Its own hot-reload doc names \"effects as the observable boundary of a UI DSL\" as a use. An extension's `perform CreateElement` is the op stream. Cost: it is a large dependency in flux, and the language's type story has to be built on its typed AST.\nB. **A small bespoke VM.** Reactive primitives and markup as opcodes, fuel built in, tiny binary, fast cold start. Cost: lexer, parser, compiler, VM and stdlib to write and keep, duplicating zyntax.\nC. **Haxe compiled to .hl and loaded by ash.** No new language, but it needs the Haxe compiler and macros at extension build time, and it is not scripting.\n\nRecommendation: A, behind the op-stream protocol, with M0 to confirm it before committing. If M0 fails, B reuses everything except the VM, because the host side only sees the protocol.\n\n\n    my-ext/\n      extension.json     name, version, description, main, capabilities, contributes\n      main.hxs           (or a compiled main.zbc)\n\n`contributes`: commands (id, title, icon), key bindings (command, key, when), settings (a schema: id, type, default, description, category), colour schemes (token sets for light and dark), status items, views (sidebar, panel, editor page), tasks, terminal profiles. Built-in features register through the same contribution points: the workspace Settings page reads one settings registry and one key binding registry, so an extension's settings and bindings appear beside the built-in ones. The Run and Debug code hints (38ca816) are a good first built-in contribution.\n\nWhere they live: `~/.rayde/extensions/` for the user, `.rayde/extensions/` for the workspace; which are enabled is in `.rayde/settings.json`.\n\n\n- Saving a script reloads it; component state stays when its layout hash matches, else the component restarts and the page says why.\n- Every system gets describe and structured errors (the standing rule): loaded extensions, contributions, granted capabilities, live element tree, op counts, fuel used, last N ops. `rayde ext check \u003cdir\u003e` compiles headless.\n- Budgets to benchmark, as for the terminal: op throughput per frame, cold start of an extension, memory per component, patch latency from event to applied ops.\n\n\nM0. Spike, one week: answer the unknowns below with numbers. Exit: a recommendation between A and B.\nM1. Host side with no VM: the op protocol, the renderer on RayDE, the tag registry for built-in tags, a run-time Tw resolver, driven by a JSON file of ops. Snapshot tests. Needs ashui (below).\nM2. Language: grammar, compile, run a counter component, events, effects, hot reload.\nM3. Manifest and contributions: commands, key bindings, settings, status items, colour schemes. The Extensions page: install from a folder, enable, disable, capabilities, errors.\nM4. Capabilities and APIs: buffers, tasks, terminal, process. Then views beyond status items.\nM5. Compiled packages, then sharing. Language providers last.\n\n\n- A run-time `Tw.apply(node, classes)` sharing the table the macro uses.\n- A run-time component registry: tag name to factory taking props and children, so a tag can be built without the macro.\n- Confirm `Identity` takes class names at run time for non-utility classes.\n\n\n1. Does zyntax's interpreter run a whole program with the `parse-only`/interpreter-only build, with no Cranelift?\n2. Cold start: grammar load and compile of a small extension. Zyntax notes a 417-rule grammar costs startup unless pre-compiled.\n3. Cost of a `perform` per element op, and of `DynamicBox` values in hot paths.\n4. Fuel or instruction budgets: not found in the docs; likely to be added upstream.\n5. Whether an HDLL cdylib that links zyntax_embed stays a reasonable size, and loads under ash as `cterm.hdll` does.\n6. Stability of zyntax's embedding API; it is the user's own project, so it can change with this.\n\n\n1. Gradual typing or fully dynamic?\n2. Language name and file extension (`.hxs`?).\n3. Should extensions be able to provide language services in the first cut, or only UI and commands?\n4. Is an extension host thread enough, or should extensions run in a separate process (stronger isolation, costs a pipe)?","files":null}]}